From 55dd79b2c081e0140f1dad56eab0e49b6ccb3920 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: leerep <leerep@163.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 14:15:54 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] luci-app-nss-overview: add thermal and NSS acceleration rows
 to the status overview

Add a self-contained LuCI add-on package which shows the SoC temperature and the QCA NSS/PPE hardware acceleration counters inside the system table of the "Status -> Overview" page.

The two rows are added through LuCI's documented extension point: luci-mod-status scans /www/luci-static/resources/view/status/include/ for *.js files, loads them sorted by name and calls load()/render() on each of them from view/status/index.js. 11_nss.js therefore sorts right after 10_system.js (the system table) and patches that table from render().

The data is collected by the bundled Lua controller from the ECM/PPE debugfs counters and the thermal zones and is fetched from load(), i.e. before index.js renders anything, so the table and the two extra rows are written to the DOM in one synchronous pass. Doing the same work from a timer or a MutationObserver after render() instead makes the rows disappear and reappear on every 5 second poll of the overview page, which shows up as a jumping page.

The counters are reported per address family: ECM keeps separate front ends for IPv4 and IPv6 (ecm_ppe_ipv{4,6}, ecm_sfe_ipv{4,6}) and the PPE flow table has separate v4_*/v6_* entries, so the controller reads both families and the front end shows the split next to the totals. The CPU temperature is read from the thermal zone the controller recognises as the CPU sensor (a zone whose type contains "cpu", falling back to tsens_tz_sensor14 on ipq5332, whose zones are renamed by ipq5332-thermald.dtsi) instead of the maximum over all sensors, and is labelled as such.

